🟠 **Severity: HIGH**

When the queue is full, `dropRIter` picks the last entry from the eviction-target account's `transactions` map via `rbegin()`. If that entry is a batch **inner** transaction (which is never inserted into `byFee_`), calling `byFee_.iterator_to()` on it is **undefined behavior** per boost::intrusive semantics, potentially causing memory corruption or a node crash. Previously unreachable because batches could not be queued.
Helpful? Add 👍 / 👎

<details>
<summary>💡 Fix Suggestion</summary>

**Suggestion:** Before calling `byFee_.iterator_to(dropRIter->second)`, check whether the selected transaction is a batch inner transaction (i.e., `dropRIter->second.parentTx` is non-null). Inner transactions are never inserted into `byFee_`, so calling `iterator_to()` on them is undefined behavior.

At line 805, after obtaining `dropRIter` via `rbegin()`, skip any batch inner transactions by walking backwards through the map. If all transactions in the account are batch inners, fall back to the `telCAN_NOT_QUEUE_FULL` path since none can be evicted via `byFee_`.

The same pattern also exists at line ~2146-2155 in `TxQ::accept()` where `account.transactions.rbegin()` is similarly used with `byFee_.iterator_to()` without checking for inner transactions — that location needs the same fix.

> **⚠️ Experimental Feature**: This code suggestion is automatically generated. Please review carefully.

```suggestion
            // The queue is full, and this transaction is more
            // valuable, so kick out the cheapest transaction.
            auto dropRIter = endAccount.transactions.rbegin();
            // Skip batch inner transactions — they are not in byFee_.
            while (dropRIter != endAccount.transactions.rend() &&
                   dropRIter->second.parentTx)
                ++dropRIter;
            if (dropRIter == endAccount.transactions.rend())
            {
                // All transactions for this account are batch inners;
                // cannot evict via byFee_.
                JLOG(j_.info())
                    << "Queue is full, and transaction " << txID_
                    << " cannot evict batch inner transactions";
                return ApplyResult{telCAN_NOT_QUEUE_FULL, false};
            }
            XRPL_ASSERT(
                dropRIter->second.account == lastRIter->account,
                "xrpl::TxQ::apply : cheapest transaction found");
            JLOG(j_.info()) << "Removing last item of account " << lastRIter->account
                            << " from queue with average fee of " << endEffectiveFeeLevel
                            << " in favor of " << txID_ << " with fee of " << feeLevelPaid_;
            txq_.erase(txq_.byFee_.iterator_to(dropRIter->second));
```

</details>

If the proposedTx payload makes a mention of `sfDelegate`, the delegated-account must authorize this transaction. A TransactionProposalSign from the actual account must be rejected with an error code. The order of these two checks need to be flipped.

Reference [documentation for Delegated transactions](https://xrpl.org/docs/references/protocol/transactions/common-fields#delegate).

[Other parts of rippled](https://github.com/XRPLF/rippled/blob/b6a899583b2da4dd03aa3fd95599d2f5f16b80ac/src/libxrpl/protocol/STTx.cpp#L662) have switched the order of checks. i.e. we first check if sfDelegate is specified in the payload of the transaction, if not, we default to the sfAccount value. 

Here is the test case that demonstrates this issue:
```
void
testDelegatedPayloadRejectsAccountSigningFor(FeatureBitset features)
{
    testcase("delegated payload rejects SigningFor = Account");

    using namespace jtx;
    using namespace std::chrono_literals;

    Env env{*this, features};

    Account const target{"target"};
    Account const dest{"dest"};
    Account const delegateAcct{"delegateAcct"};
    Account const ds1{"ds1"};
    env.fund(XRP(10000), target, dest, delegateAcct, ds1);
    env.close();

    env(delegate::set(target, delegateAcct, {"Payment"}));
    env(signers(delegateAcct, 1, {{ds1, 1}}));
    env.close();

    std::uint32_t const ticketSeq = proposal::createTicket(env, target);
    json::Value tx = pay(target, dest, XRP(1));
    tx[sfDelegate.jsonName] = delegateAcct.human();
    env(proposal::create(
        target,
        proposal::unsignedPayload(env, tx, ticketSeq, /*extraSigners=*/1),
        proposal::expiration(env, 100s)));
    env.close();

    // The Delegate authorizes this payload; the Account does not. Its
    // contribution must be refused rather than written into the payload's
    // own signature slot, where it would lock the Delegate out for good.
    env(proposal::sign(env, target, target, ticketSeq, target, target),
        Ter(tecNO_PERMISSION));
    env.close();

    {
        auto const sle = proposal::entry(env, target, ticketSeq);
        if (!BEAST_EXPECT(sle))
            return;
        auto const stored = sle->getFieldObject(sfProposedTransaction);
        BEAST_EXPECT(stored.getFieldVL(sfSigningPubKey).empty());
        BEAST_EXPECT(!stored.isFieldPresent(sfTxnSignature));
    }

    // The Delegate's signer is still able to contribute and complete it.
    env(proposal::sign(env, ds1, target, ticketSeq, delegateAcct, ds1));
    env.close();

    env(proposedJson(env, target, ticketSeq), Sig(kNone));
    env.close();

    BEAST_EXPECT(!proposal::entry(env, target, ticketSeq));
    BEAST_EXPECT(env.balance(dest) == XRP(10000) + XRP(1));
}
```

⚪ **Severity: LOW**

For batch inner transactions with a `sfDelegate`, `isRequiredSigningFor` accepts **both** the inner Account and the Delegate as required signers. When delegation is active only the Delegate should sign; accepting the Account may allow recording a contribution that the batch submit-path verification will ultimately reject.
Helpful? Add 👍 / 👎

<details>
<summary>💡 Fix Suggestion</summary>

**Suggestion:** Mirror the `isOuterSigningFor` logic for batch inner transactions: when `sfDelegate` is present on an inner transaction, only the delegate should be accepted as the required signer — the Account must be excluded. This is consistent with `isOuterSigningFor` (lines 49-55) and `STTx::getInitiator`, which both return only the delegate when `sfDelegate` is present. Replace the OR condition that accepts both Account and Delegate with an if/else that prioritizes `sfDelegate` when present.

> **⚠️ Experimental Feature**: This code suggestion is automatically generated. Please review carefully.

```suggestion
    return std::ranges::any_of(proposedTx.getFieldArray(sfRawTransactions), [&](auto const& inner) {
        auto const tx = innerTxn(inner);
        if (signingFor == outer)
            return false;
        // Mirror isOuterSigningFor: when sfDelegate is present, only the
        // delegate is the required signer, not the Account.
        if (tx.isFieldPresent(sfDelegate))
            return tx.getAccountID(sfDelegate) == signingFor;
        return tx.isFieldPresent(sfAccount) && tx.getAccountID(sfAccount) == signingFor;
    });
```

</details>

This design does not allow an inner-batch transaction (say Account-Outer) to fund a new account (say Account-Fresh) AND a subsequent inner-batch transaction to be initiated from Account-fresh. This feature is allowed in the normally submitted batch transactions.

`permitPhantom` must be set to `true`, in case of all the inner-batch-transactions, in the single-sign case also. (this is missing in this implementation)

The below test captures the expectations of phantom inner accounts in a Batch transaction:

```
void
testBatchInnerFromUncreatedAccount(FeatureBitset features)
{
    testcase("batch inner from an account an earlier inner creates");

    using namespace jtx;
    using namespace std::chrono_literals;

    Env env{*this, features};

    Account const outer{"outer"};
    Account const dest{"dest"};
    Account const fresh{"fresh"};  // never funded: the batch creates it
    env.fund(XRP(10000), outer, dest);
    env.close();
    BEAST_EXPECT(!env.le(keylet::account(fresh.id())));

    std::uint32_t const ticketSeq = proposal::createTicket(env, outer);
    json::Value const proposedTx = proposal::unsignedBatch(
        env,
        outer,
        ticketSeq,
        tfAllOrNothing,
        {proposal::innerTx(pay(outer, fresh, XRP(1000)), env.seq(outer)),
         proposal::innerTx(pay(fresh, dest, XRP(1)), env.current()->seq())},
        /*numSigners=*/1);

    env(proposal::create(outer, proposedTx, proposal::expiration(env, 100s)));
    env.close();

    // Batch::checkBatchSign authorizes an inner from a not-yet-created
    // account with that account's own master key
    // (permitUncreatedAccount=true), so this is a contribution the
    // completed Batch will accept and it must be recordable here.
    env(proposal::sign(env, outer, outer, ticketSeq, fresh, fresh));
    env.close();

    auto const sle = proposal::entry(env, outer, ticketSeq);
    if (!BEAST_EXPECT(sle))
        return;
    auto const stored = sle->getFieldObject(sfProposedTransaction);
    BEAST_EXPECT(stored.isFieldPresent(sfBatchSigners));
    auto const& batchSigners = stored.getFieldArray(sfBatchSigners);
    BEAST_EXPECT(batchSigners.size() == 1);
    BEAST_EXPECT(batchSigners[0].getAccountID(sfAccount) == fresh.id());
}
```

I think the function can be refactored to reuse checkSingleSign? Example:

 ```
  TER
checkSignerKey(
    ReadView const& view,
    AccountID const& signerAccount,
    Slice const& signingPubKey,
    bool const permitPhantom,
    beast::Journal j)
{
    // Defensive: preclaim already ran verify(), which fails on an unparseable
    // key, so a caller from that path never reaches this branch.
    // LCOV_EXCL_START
    if (!publicKeyType(signingPubKey))
    {
        JLOG(j.debug()) << "TransactionProposalSign: unknown key type.";
        return temBAD_SIGNATURE;
    }
    // LCOV_EXCL_STOP

    auto const fromKey = calcAccountID(PublicKey(signingPubKey));
    auto const sleSigner = view.read(keylet::account(signerAccount));
    if (!sleSigner)
    {
        if (permitPhantom && fromKey == signerAccount)
            return tesSUCCESS;
        // Single-sign path: signerAccount is SigningFor, which is the
        // proposed transaction's target and was verified to exist at
        // TransactionProposalCreate time.
        if (fromKey == signerAccount)
            return tecNO_PERMISSION;  // LCOV_EXCL_LINE
        JLOG(j.debug()) << "TransactionProposalSign: key does not match "
                           "master or regular key.";
        return tecNO_PERMISSION;
    }

    if (auto const ret =
            Transactor::checkSingleSign(view, fromKey, signerAccount, sleSigner, j);
        !isTesSuccess(ret))
    {
        if (ret == tefMASTER_DISABLED)
        {
            JLOG(j.debug()) << "TransactionProposalSign: master key disabled.";
        }
        else
        {
            JLOG(j.debug()) << "TransactionProposalSign: key does not match "
                               "master or regular key.";
        }
        return tecNO_PERMISSION;
    }
    return tesSUCCESS;
}
